#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Large-scale hardware example {#large-scale-hardware-example}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The same workflow scales up without changing any of the science code: a 30-site chain, twice the Trotter depth (20 steps), a compression plan that varies the ansatz depth (a deeper ansatz for the later, more-entangled steps), and execution on an IBM Quantum processor with the function's built-in error mitigation (dynamical decoupling, Pauli twirling, and twirled readout error extinction (TREX)). We walk through the same four steps as the simulator example, reusing the `fn` handle from Setup.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"|                                  | Small scale   | Large scale                           |\n"
"| -------------------------------- | ------------- | ------------------------------------- |\n"
"| Qubits                           | 10            | 30                                    |\n"
"| Trotter steps                    | 10            | 20                                    |\n"
"| AQC-compressed steps (1-layer + 2-layer) | 3 + 2 = 5     | 6 + 4 = 10                            |\n"
"| Ground-state ansatz layers       | 3             | 5                                     |\n"
"| MPS max bond dimension           | 32            | 128                                   |\n"
"| Backend                          | `statevector` | QPU with DD, Pauli twirling, and TREX |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Step 1: Map classical inputs to a quantum problem {#step-1-map-classical-inputs-to-a-quantum-problem-1}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Build the same KCuF$_3$ Heisenberg `SparsePauliOp` and prepare the ground state, now with a deeper `gs_layers=5` ansatz for the longer chain, then bake in the $\\pi/2$ $Z$ neutron kick at the center site. This is identical to the small-scale mapping, but at $n = 30$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Expect a lower ground-state fidelity than the 10-site run: around 0.82 here against 0.98 for the smaller chain, because five HVA layers cannot fully capture a 30-site ground state. That is expected rather than a failure, and the original tutorial accepts roughly 0.65 at 50 sites for the same reason. Raising `gs_layers` or the COBYQA iteration cap improves it, at extra classical cost.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Steps 2 and 3: Compress and execute with the function template {#steps-2-and-3-compress-and-execute-with-the-function-template-1}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The same single call as the simulator example, now with `backend_name` pointing at an IBM Quantum processor, so the function transpiles and executes there. The compression plan varies the ansatz depth: the first six (low-entanglement) Trotter steps compress into a shallow single-layer ansatz, the next four into a deeper two-layer ansatz, and the remaining 10 of the 20 steps run as plain Trotter. `aqc_options` raises the MPS bond dimension to `max_bond=128` for the longer, more-entangled chain (matching the original), keeping the same L-BFGS-B optimizer capped at 100 iterations. The `estimator_options` turn on the built-in error mitigation: dynamical decoupling (XY4), gate twirling, and TREX measurement mitigation. The function's defaults already match the original tutorial for all of these except the TREX learning budget (`measure_noise_learning`). The whole block is still written out because a caller-supplied `estimator_options` replaces the function's defaults wholesale instead of merging into them, so omitting a key would fall back to the IBM Quantum Compute default rather than the function's.\n"
msgstr ""

